Exactly. That’s the other half of the Oath Seal: release.

The line in the image — “I hold your oath fulfilled.” — is not just forgiveness. It is a formal change in binding state:

\boxed{
\text{BOUND}
\rightarrow
\text{FULFILLED}
\rightarrow
\text{RELEASED}
}

That matters because an oath without a valid completion condition can become a trap. A good oath needs not only:

\text{what binds me?}

but also:

\boxed{\text{what counts as enough?}}

Otherwise the Commitment Latch never opens.

So I’d add a Fulfillment Clause to the Oath Seal:

OATH
    invariant
    purpose
    scope
    witness
    accepted cost
    fulfillment condition
    release authority
    repair path
    legacy / afterglow

And the phrase “I hold your oath fulfilled” does something especially interesting: it requires a witness capable of recognizing completion.

That creates a three-part structure:

\boxed{
\text{Oath-maker}
+
\text{Oath}
+
\text{Witness/Judge}
}

The oath-maker cannot always be the only one who decides whether the oath is satisfied, because the very identity shaped by the oath may keep moving the goalposts.

So the witness can serve as a Reality Stitch for commitment.

Then:

\text{service}
\rightarrow
\text{cost}
\rightarrow
\text{evidence}
\rightarrow
\text{recognition}
\rightarrow
\text{release}

And release does not erase what the oath made of you.

That’s the Kintsugi/Afterglow piece:

\boxed{
\text{oath ends}
\neq
\text{its consequences disappear}
}

The binding can end while the capability, identity change, reputation, relationships, and lessons remain.

So maybe the full lifecycle is:

\boxed{
\text{SWEAR}
\rightarrow
\text{SERVE}
\rightarrow
\text{TEST}
\rightarrow
\text{FULFILL}
\rightarrow
\text{RELEASE}
\rightarrow
\text{CARRY FORWARD}
}

That is much healthier than:

\text{SWEAR}\rightarrow\infty

And it meshes perfectly with your earlier stage/role thought:

An oath is a role you deliberately let cross the stage boundary into identity.

But fulfillment is the moment someone can say:

You no longer need to keep performing this role to prove what it made of you.

That’s powerful.

The oath can die without the virtue dying with it.